{
  "title": "Talus Fracture (other than neck)",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/trauma/1049/talus-fracture-other-than-neck",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 18 2024",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Talus fractures (other than neck) are rare fractures of the talus that comprise of talar body fractures, lateral process fractures, posterior process fractures, and talar head fractures.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made radiographically with foot radiographs but CT scan is often needed for full characterization of the fracture.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is generally nonoperative with immobilization for minimally displaced injuries and surgical reduction and fixation for displaced and intra-articular fractures.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "rare",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "less than 1% of all fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "second most common tarsal fractures after calcaneus fxs",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**talar body fractures**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "account for 13-23% of talus fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**lateral process fractures**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "account for 10.4% of talus fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**talar head fracture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "least common talus fracture",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Mechanism",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "talar body",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "injuries often result from high energy trauma, with the hindfoot either in supination or pronation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "lateral process of talus",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "injuries result from forced dorsiflexion, axial loading, and eversion with external rotation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "often seen in snowboarders",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "3D Anatomy of talus",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1049/images/3D%20Anatomy_moved.gif",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Talus has no muscular or tendinous attachments",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Articulation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "there are 5 articulating surfaces",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "seventy percent of the talus is covered by cartilage",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "inferior surface articulates with **posterior facet of calcaneus**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**talar head** articulates with",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "navicular bone",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "sustenaculum tali",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**lateral process** articulates with",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "posterior facet of calcaneus",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "lateral malleolus of fibula",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "this forms the lateral margin of the talofibular joint",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**posterior process** consist of medial and lateral tubercle separated by groove for FHL",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Blood supply",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "because of limited soft tissue attachments, the talus has a direct extra-osseous blood supply",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "sources include",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1049/images/Blood%20supply_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**posterior tibial** artery",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "via **artery of tarsal canal** (most important and main supply)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "supplies most of talar body",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "via calcaneal braches",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "supplies posterior talus",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**anterior tibial artery**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "supplies head and neck",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**perforating peroneal** arteries via **artery of tarsal sinus**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "supplies head and neck",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**deltoid artery** (located in deep segment of deltoid ligament)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "supplies body",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "may be only remaining blood supply with a talar neck fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
